00:22:10.460 --> 00:22:12.290
and obviously this is a net America,

00:22:12.290 --> 00:22:15.130
there's obviously Mississippi has not been

00:22:15.130 --> 00:22:17.130
crushing it for the
last three generations,

00:22:17.130 --> 00:22:18.910
or most of it unfortunately,

00:22:18.910 --> 00:22:21.050
which is a whole other
obsession of mine, Mississippi,

00:22:21.050 --> 00:22:23.083
but I haven't really talked about it yet.

00:22:25.226 --> 00:22:28.380
I'm very fascinated by parents

00:22:28.380 --> 00:22:31.013
trying to protect kids from using,

00:22:31.980 --> 00:22:33.830
like in a big way.

00:22:33.830 --> 00:22:36.560
Like, especially now that I'm a parent,

00:22:36.560 --> 00:22:39.407
just watching parents
talk about things like,

00:22:39.407 --> 00:22:42.100
"We have to invite everybody
to the birthday party,"

00:22:42.100 --> 00:22:44.870
you know, just like stuff
that is so ridiculous to me.

00:22:44.870 --> 00:22:47.830
As three competitors,
and it sounds like you

00:22:47.830 --> 00:22:49.390
started athletics super early and all,

00:22:49.390 --> 00:22:52.590
but I'm projecting but I
think you did as well, right?

00:22:52.590 --> 00:22:55.730
Do you feel like that was
a great thing for you?

00:22:55.730 --> 00:22:57.580
Did your parents try to actually

00:22:57.580 --> 00:23:00.120
manipulate some not losing,

00:23:00.120 --> 00:23:01.030
did you see it?

00:23:01.030 --> 00:23:04.420
What is your three opinions
on this notion very simply,

00:23:04.420 --> 00:23:06.710
that losing a lot young is actually

00:23:06.710 --> 00:23:08.580
the great advantage to life,

00:23:08.580 --> 00:23:10.190
not a disadvantage,

00:23:10.190 --> 00:23:14.050
that I think parents have unbelievably,

00:23:14.050 --> 00:23:15.830
tried to protect kids from losing,

00:23:15.830 --> 00:23:19.690
we've changed so much of
our athletics in the US.

00:23:19.690 --> 00:23:21.430
We don't have keeping score until,

00:23:21.430 --> 00:23:23.250
like, fucking fifth grade.

00:23:23.250 --> 00:23:25.253
It's real intense.

00:23:27.070 --> 00:23:29.330
It's a very interesting conversation

00:23:29.330 --> 00:23:31.560
and, to me, you start talking
about unwinding things,

00:23:31.560 --> 00:23:36.000
look, kids have to unwind
alcoholism and abuse

00:23:36.000 --> 00:23:38.670
and a lot of hardcore stuff that you can't

00:23:39.570 --> 00:23:41.600
predict or things of that nature.

00:23:41.600 --> 00:23:43.670
Watching the country,
and I'm talking about

00:23:43.670 --> 00:23:45.150
the liberal and the conservative,

00:23:45.150 --> 00:23:46.620
I love when I bring this up,

00:23:46.620 --> 00:23:49.047
a lot of the homies from Alabama are like,

00:23:49.047 --> 00:23:50.977
"Yeah, you fucking New York and LA homies.

00:23:50.977 --> 00:23:52.117
"Gary, go them."

00:23:53.465 --> 00:23:54.298
I'm weird.

00:23:54.298 --> 00:23:56.397
When somebody hits me
up on Twitter and says,

00:23:56.397 --> 00:23:57.247
"Yeah, you're so right.

00:23:57.247 --> 00:23:58.777
"The New York, LA liberal shit.

00:23:58.777 --> 00:24:00.270
"Get them, Gary, I love you,"

00:24:00.270 --> 00:24:01.217
I look at their account and I'm like,

00:24:01.217 --> 00:24:03.090
"Okay, this town in Arkansas,"

00:24:03.090 --> 00:24:06.710
I like Google and look up the
rules of their soccer league

00:24:06.710 --> 00:24:07.890
and then like reply to them,

00:24:07.890 --> 00:24:09.427
be like, "Yo, dude, your league has

00:24:09.427 --> 00:24:11.720
"fucking 8th place trophies, too."

00:24:11.720 --> 00:24:14.293
So this is, like, a net thing.

00:24:14.293 --> 00:24:16.640
What is your two cents, three cents

00:24:16.640 --> 00:24:18.290
on this whole conversation?

00:24:18.290 --> 00:24:20.930
- Yeah, I mean, I 1000% agree.

00:24:20.930 --> 00:24:23.360
I mean, like the way I was brought up

00:24:23.360 --> 00:24:26.835
it was like either you're
a winner or you're a loser,

00:24:26.835 --> 00:24:28.340
and it worked.

00:24:28.340 --> 00:24:32.040
I mean, for me, too, I
remember I used to cry.

00:24:32.040 --> 00:24:35.180
I was so emotional about
getting second place

00:24:36.620 --> 00:24:39.713
because it was either you win or you lose.

00:24:41.001 --> 00:24:43.213
- And how did your parents
deal with the crying?

00:24:45.180 --> 00:24:47.040
- They were like, "Well, you didn't win."

00:24:47.040 --> 00:24:47.873
- [Gary] Right, "Cry."

00:24:47.873 --> 00:24:49.160
- Yeah, they were like, "Yeah,"

00:24:49.160 --> 00:24:51.460
you know, "do better next time."

00:24:51.460 --> 00:24:54.750
- I cried basically minimally once a week

00:24:54.750 --> 00:24:57.140
for the first 13 years of my life.

00:24:57.140 --> 00:24:57.973
This is not a joke.

00:24:57.973 --> 00:24:59.930
I would cry all the time.

00:24:59.930 --> 00:25:02.260
I just cried every time I lost.

00:25:02.260 --> 00:25:04.860
Until I was 9, I cried every time I lost.

00:25:04.860 --> 00:25:08.400
Like, dead tears, like death tears.

00:25:08.400 --> 00:25:10.520
To the point where nobody
really wanted to play with me.

00:25:10.520 --> 00:25:12.055
(laughing)

00:25:12.055 --> 00:25:12.888
I'm being serious.

00:25:12.888 --> 00:25:14.730
And I could tell when
people were letting me win,

00:25:14.730 --> 00:25:15.780
and that would make me cry, too.

00:25:15.780 --> 00:25:17.190
So I was just crying.

00:25:17.190 --> 00:25:18.600
- Yeah, no, and for me, too.

00:25:18.600 --> 00:25:20.870
I thought it was very interesting

00:25:20.870 --> 00:25:23.340
when I was a young kid because I was,

00:25:23.340 --> 00:25:26.230
like, obsessed with getting
first in every event

00:25:26.230 --> 00:25:28.710
and when I would go over
to my friend's houses

00:25:28.710 --> 00:25:32.640
that had participation trophies
and trophies in their room,

00:25:32.640 --> 00:25:34.668
and I was always like, "What?

00:25:34.668 --> 00:25:36.307
"What place did you get?"

00:25:37.300 --> 00:25:39.270
- Did they think you
were too much, seriously?

00:25:39.270 --> 00:25:41.029
- [Anastasia] Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:25:41.029 --> 00:25:44.080
- Did you have to learn how
to coddle that a little bit?

00:25:44.080 --> 00:25:45.330
Because I definitely did.

00:25:46.200 --> 00:25:49.890
- When I was 12, 13, I was
doing shoots for Teen Magazine

00:25:49.890 --> 00:25:53.560
and I was like coming up and
getting attention and stuff,

00:25:53.560 --> 00:25:55.210
and that was also weird, too.

00:25:55.210 --> 00:25:57.347
I wasn't bullied but people
were definitely like--

00:25:57.347 --> 00:25:59.940
- "You think you're special."

00:25:59.940 --> 00:26:02.450
- Well, and also, too,
when I was growing up,

00:26:02.450 --> 00:26:04.360
surfing wasn't cool for girls,

00:26:04.360 --> 00:26:05.410
so that's a whole other thing,

00:26:05.410 --> 00:26:09.737
but it was like, "Oh, you're like a dude.

00:26:09.737 --> 00:26:13.683
"You're like a tomboy, you're not cute."

00:26:13.683 --> 00:26:14.516
You know?

00:26:14.516 --> 00:26:16.097
It was a lot of that stuff but yeah.

00:26:16.097 --> 00:26:18.652
- Did you care or were you so deep into

00:26:18.652 --> 00:26:19.853
surfing that you didn't give a fuck?

00:26:19.853 --> 00:26:21.270
Or did you cry?

00:26:21.270 --> 00:26:22.550
- No, that never made me cry.

00:26:22.550 --> 00:26:23.510
Losing made me cry.

00:26:23.510 --> 00:26:24.720
- [Gary] Love it, what about for you?

00:26:24.720 --> 00:26:25.840
- I think it starts with parenting.

00:26:25.840 --> 00:26:27.650
I mean, I almost didn't
play college lacrosse

00:26:27.650 --> 00:26:28.483
because I wasn't recruited,

00:26:28.483 --> 00:26:30.440
I felt like I didn't have
many options to go to school.

00:26:30.440 --> 00:26:32.330
At one point, I had no options.

00:26:32.330 --> 00:26:33.780
My fifth year of high school,

00:26:33.780 --> 00:26:34.613
trying to go somewhere,

00:26:34.613 --> 00:26:36.350
but I think it was because of how my--

00:26:36.350 --> 00:26:37.710
- [Gary] How'd you end up in
a fifth year in high school?

00:26:37.710 --> 00:26:38.830
- I wasn't recruited so I took a

00:26:38.830 --> 00:26:40.720
post-graduate at a prep school.

00:26:40.720 --> 00:26:41.790
- So you were that hardcore?

00:26:41.790 --> 00:26:44.460
And that's where I
thought you were gonna go.

00:26:44.460 --> 00:26:45.637
So you were that religious of, like,

00:26:45.637 --> 00:26:46.920
"Fuck this, I'm playing lacrosse."

00:26:46.920 --> 00:26:48.430
- Yeah, at least that's where my dad,

00:26:48.430 --> 00:26:51.770
when I was younger it was all about being,

00:26:51.770 --> 00:26:53.190
you know, the best you could be

00:26:53.190 --> 00:26:54.770
and being the best and winning,

00:26:54.770 --> 00:26:57.540
and I was good when I was younger.

00:26:57.540 --> 00:27:00.500
- That's such an intense gear.

00:27:00.500 --> 00:27:03.580
Like, I can't even think of anybody I know

00:27:03.580 --> 00:27:06.330
that took a fucking fifth
year of high school.

00:27:06.330 --> 00:27:08.190
To me, why didn't you accept that

00:27:08.190 --> 00:27:10.190
the market said you weren't good enough?

00:27:11.320 --> 00:27:13.040
Because it was still one more option

00:27:13.040 --> 00:27:15.453
and you needed to,
like, scratch that itch?

00:27:20.261 --> 00:27:22.594
I mean this when I say this,

00:27:24.060 --> 00:27:25.950
I'd love for anybody to shoot their hat,

00:27:25.950 --> 00:27:28.590
I don't even know anybody
who ever took a fifth year

00:27:28.590 --> 00:27:30.390
of high school to get into a sport.

00:27:30.390 --> 00:27:33.530
Like, that's an intense jump.

00:27:33.530 --> 00:27:38.530
- Yeah, I love lacrosse, I
had that dream of playing

00:27:38.830 --> 00:27:41.060
Division One lacrosse at
the highest level possible,

00:27:41.060 --> 00:27:43.900
playing on Memorial Day in
the National Championship,

00:27:43.900 --> 00:27:45.537
and when that wasn't happening I was like,

00:27:45.537 --> 00:27:47.580
"Okay, what do I need to do
differently to be better?"

00:27:47.580 --> 00:27:49.560
And I kept working, working,

00:27:49.560 --> 00:27:50.890
put so much time and effort into it.

00:27:50.890 --> 00:27:52.720
- When did it set in
that wasn't happening?

00:27:52.720 --> 00:27:53.883
Late junior year?

00:27:57.682 --> 00:28:00.645
- I was recruited at, like,
Division Three, Division Two.

00:28:00.645 --> 00:28:02.895
- But we're not talking
Hopkins and Syracuse.

00:28:03.750 --> 00:28:06.020
- I'd say my junior to senior year,

00:28:06.020 --> 00:28:08.273
I was committed to a Division One school,

00:28:08.273 --> 00:28:13.273
I was outside the top
fifty and I was like,

00:28:13.287 --> 00:28:15.737
"Okay, this is my last
push to make this happen."

00:28:18.762 --> 00:28:20.959
- Like junior year going to senior year,

00:28:20.959 --> 00:28:21.792
you had a hedge and you were like,

00:28:21.792 --> 00:28:24.231
"But I'm gonna fucking dismantle it

00:28:24.231 --> 00:28:26.970
"and somebody's gonna
see that and swoop in."

00:28:26.970 --> 00:28:28.300
- Yeah, and then I did that.

00:28:28.300 --> 00:28:30.260
I just said, "I'm not coming,"

00:28:30.260 --> 00:28:32.370
and I took a fifth year
at the prep school.

00:28:32.370 --> 00:28:33.470
- So you did that senior year,

00:28:33.470 --> 00:28:35.320
and that didn't happen, right?

00:28:35.320 --> 00:28:37.450
Your top ten school didn't come in.

00:28:37.450 --> 00:28:40.470
- I actually made this
decision after my senior year.

00:28:40.470 --> 00:28:42.080
I made it in the summer after senior year,

00:28:42.080 --> 00:28:43.437
I said, "I'm not coming."

00:28:43.437 --> 00:28:44.270
- To this school.

00:28:44.270 --> 00:28:45.103
- [Rob] Yeah.

00:28:45.103 --> 00:28:47.270
- And you said, "I'm gonna
go to this prep school."

00:28:47.270 --> 00:28:48.263
And that's allowed?

00:28:49.830 --> 00:28:51.181
- Yeah.

00:28:51.181 --> 00:28:52.098
Not really.

00:28:53.752 --> 00:28:57.100
I signed a contract, I
was supposed to be there,

00:28:57.100 --> 00:29:00.590
so technically I could only
look at non-scholarship schools.

00:29:00.590 --> 00:29:01.423
- Oh, that part.

00:29:01.423 --> 00:29:02.370
I mean like prep school.

00:29:03.629 --> 00:29:04.755
So what ended up happening?

00:29:04.755 --> 00:29:07.400
You go to the prep
school and what happens?

00:29:07.400 --> 00:29:08.840
- I was talking to a few schools,

00:29:08.840 --> 00:29:10.860
they all told me they
didn't have room for me,

00:29:10.860 --> 00:29:11.780
couldn't get me in,

00:29:11.780 --> 00:29:14.560
and at one point in October,

00:29:14.560 --> 00:29:16.690
the reason I was there,
play regional lacrosse,

00:29:16.690 --> 00:29:18.430
I was dunking anyone.

00:29:18.430 --> 00:29:22.260
And then out of nowhere Cornell came and--

00:29:22.260 --> 00:29:24.735
- You have to be registered
every year or how did it--

00:29:24.735 --> 00:29:26.283
- I'll be there next year.

00:29:26.283 --> 00:29:29.770
It was crazy, I remember
calling around crying

00:29:29.770 --> 00:29:31.930
when these two other schools
told me they couldn't take me.

00:29:31.930 --> 00:29:34.280
I was like, "What am I here for?"

00:29:34.280 --> 00:29:35.180
- [Gary] At this prep school.

00:29:35.180 --> 00:29:36.650
- Yeah, "I'm here, I wanna play lacrosse."

00:29:36.650 --> 00:29:38.214
- I'm a fifth year senior.

00:29:38.214 --> 00:29:41.445
- "I don't own a car, I can't
go out, I can't leave campus."

00:29:41.445 --> 00:29:42.400
- [Gary] Where were you?

00:29:42.400 --> 00:29:44.070
- Deerfield Academy in Massachusetts.

00:29:44.070 --> 00:29:45.220
- So you're in Mass.

00:29:45.220 --> 00:29:46.720
- [Rob] Yeah, Deerfield, Mass.

00:29:48.000 --> 00:29:49.900
- So you're in Deerfield, Mass.

00:29:49.900 --> 00:29:51.590
All you're friends are literally, like,

00:29:51.590 --> 00:29:54.470
six, eight weeks into their college lives.

00:29:54.470 --> 00:29:56.130
You're in this high school.

00:29:56.130 --> 00:29:58.610
- I'm in a single dorm room with the

00:29:58.610 --> 00:30:00.010
center of the football team.

00:30:01.210 --> 00:30:04.270
They added a bed for me to get in there.

00:30:04.270 --> 00:30:05.750
It was a high school, yeah.

00:30:05.750 --> 00:30:06.770
Just like, "What am I doing?"

00:30:06.770 --> 00:30:08.850
- And when did Cornell call?

00:30:08.850 --> 00:30:11.060
- November, I went up for homecoming

00:30:11.060 --> 00:30:13.710
and hit it off with the coaching staff.

00:30:13.710 --> 00:30:14.690
The coach called me back,

00:30:14.690 --> 00:30:15.523
he's like, "Do you wanna come?"

00:30:15.523 --> 00:30:17.167
I was like, "Well, I have no other options

00:30:17.167 --> 00:30:19.040
"so yeah, let's do it."

00:30:19.040 --> 00:30:21.140
And I played the National Championship

00:30:21.140 --> 00:30:22.030
my freshmen year at Cornell.

00:30:22.030 --> 00:30:23.860
- That's fucking crazy.

00:30:23.860 --> 00:30:27.140
So literally in October you were a

00:30:27.140 --> 00:30:31.730
fifth year senior in wherever fucking Mass

00:30:31.730 --> 00:30:33.220
and literally that spring/summer,

00:30:33.220 --> 00:30:35.700
you're in the NCAA tournament,

00:30:35.700 --> 00:30:36.950
playing for the National Championship.

00:30:36.950 --> 00:30:38.210
- [Rob] Yeah, crazy.

00:30:38.210 --> 00:30:40.380
- That was an intense eight months.

00:30:40.380 --> 00:30:41.420
- Oh, yeah.

00:30:41.420 --> 00:30:42.830
- So what did you see around you

00:30:42.830 --> 00:30:45.963
in this highly competitive
northeast environment?

00:30:46.806 --> 00:30:48.753
You just saw a lot of parents fucking up?

00:30:49.610 --> 00:30:50.443
Or did you even realize it was happening?

00:30:50.443 --> 00:30:51.743
You were a kid.

00:30:53.076 --> 00:30:54.650
Did you see dads yelling,

00:30:54.650 --> 00:30:57.340
just like lack of self-awareness?

00:30:57.340 --> 00:30:59.730
Like a kid that you knew sucked shit

00:30:59.730 --> 00:31:01.430
in junior high that had no prayer

00:31:01.430 --> 00:31:03.830
because he didn't have the
size or the speed or the skill,

00:31:03.830 --> 00:31:05.560
but his dad, when he came over,

00:31:05.560 --> 00:31:07.490
literally thinks his
son's gonna be at Hopkins?

00:31:07.490 --> 00:31:09.690
- I think you see it today more than ever.

00:31:09.690 --> 00:31:11.280
- [Gary] Why?

00:31:11.280 --> 00:31:12.113
From your perspective.

00:31:12.113 --> 00:31:14.020
- Yeah, I mean, everything
that you touched on,

00:31:14.020 --> 00:31:17.010
I think it's just like
that notion that parents,

00:31:17.010 --> 00:31:20.020
that they want their kids to think that--

00:31:20.020 --> 00:31:20.960
They wanna be positive,

00:31:20.960 --> 00:31:22.060
think that they're gonna
be this great thing.

00:31:22.060 --> 00:31:25.200
I would have like five
goals in youth league

00:31:25.200 --> 00:31:26.160
and I would come off the field,

00:31:26.160 --> 00:31:28.487
my dad would be like, "You
missed this one on the crease,

00:31:28.487 --> 00:31:30.540
"and you missed this guy
who was wide open there."

00:31:30.540 --> 00:31:33.570
- Do you feel like that
can go far the other way?

00:31:33.570 --> 00:31:34.943
- I feel like that's why I'm here today,

00:31:34.943 --> 00:31:36.813
is because my dad was so critical of me.

00:31:39.070 --> 00:31:41.970
- Do you think if your
dad was sitting here,

00:31:41.970 --> 00:31:43.897
that if I asked him, "Hey, bro,

00:31:43.897 --> 00:31:47.237
"do you think that you, dad,
would've had the self-awareness

00:31:47.237 --> 00:31:50.037
"that your son wasn't
dealing with that well,

00:31:50.037 --> 00:31:51.167
"that you would've been able to

00:31:51.167 --> 00:31:52.280
"take the foot off the pedal,"

00:31:52.280 --> 00:31:54.470
or do you think your dad
had one move the other way.

00:31:54.470 --> 00:31:58.120
Because I think most
parents have one move,

00:31:58.120 --> 00:31:59.330
one way or the other,

00:31:59.330 --> 00:32:01.110
and I'm most fascinated by the parent

00:32:01.110 --> 00:32:03.160
that has the ability to go to the middle

00:32:03.160 --> 00:32:05.170
when they react to the reality

00:32:05.170 --> 00:32:06.720
versus their own ambition?

00:32:06.720 --> 00:32:07.670
- I think he would've.

00:32:07.670 --> 00:32:09.630
I think he saw how I reacted to it

00:32:09.630 --> 00:32:12.480
and I was competitive,
it was positive for me,

00:32:12.480 --> 00:32:14.440
and then eventually when
I got to high school--

00:32:14.440 --> 00:32:16.970
- You had friends that had dads or moms

00:32:16.970 --> 00:32:19.720
doing the same thing
that crumbled under that

00:32:19.720 --> 00:32:21.238
and wanted to punch
their parent in the face.

00:32:21.238 --> 00:32:22.723
- Absolutely, absolutely.

00:32:23.810 --> 00:32:25.637
- Yeah, my mom was a
professional tennis player.

00:32:25.637 --> 00:32:27.630
She lost in the semifinals of Wimbledon,

00:32:27.630 --> 00:32:28.860
like did the thing.

00:32:28.860 --> 00:32:31.040
- Your mom lost--

00:32:31.040 --> 00:32:31.980
- To Billy Jean King,

00:32:31.980 --> 00:32:34.190
semifinals of Wimbledon in '68, I think.

00:32:34.190 --> 00:32:36.020
- I have to look that
up as you're talking.

00:32:36.020 --> 00:32:37.690
- Yeah, Cathleen Harder.

00:32:37.690 --> 00:32:38.840
- [Gary] That's insane.

00:32:41.180 --> 00:32:45.040
- She was the real deal and
so when I was playing ball,

00:32:45.040 --> 00:32:45.970
I was a basketball player--

00:32:45.970 --> 00:32:47.542
- You're a hundredth of
the athlete your mom is.

00:32:47.542 --> 00:32:48.375
- Yeah, totally.

00:32:48.375 --> 00:32:50.833
Yeah, she could beat me in
tennis until I was like 32.

00:32:51.965 --> 00:32:53.715
And that was only because mobility.

00:32:55.770 --> 00:32:57.966
Strokes, she's still better,

00:32:57.966 --> 00:32:58.799
and she would just laugh at me,

00:32:58.799 --> 00:33:01.470
but I think her experience
in knowing that,

00:33:01.470 --> 00:33:04.470
I mean, losing is a part of
life and she understood that,

00:33:04.470 --> 00:33:05.797
and it was just how you deal with it

00:33:05.797 --> 00:33:07.350
and what you take from it,

00:33:07.350 --> 00:33:10.310
but her love never
wavered when I won or lost

00:33:10.310 --> 00:33:12.030
and I can't say the same for my dad.

00:33:12.030 --> 00:33:13.680
My dad was never a great athlete

00:33:13.680 --> 00:33:17.040
and he was more ego-invested
in my performance,

00:33:17.040 --> 00:33:22.040
and I think with him it was a lot tougher

00:33:22.120 --> 00:33:23.660
because he didn't really understand

00:33:23.660 --> 00:33:25.560
what it was like to play at a high level

00:33:25.560 --> 00:33:27.750
and his attitude towards me changed.

00:33:27.750 --> 00:33:29.980
If I had a bad game or
if I missed some shots,

00:33:29.980 --> 00:33:32.480
you know, then it was
like, "Where'd dad go?"

00:33:32.480 --> 00:33:34.930
Like, "Dad doesn't give a shit
about me anymore," you know?

00:33:34.930 --> 00:33:36.820
Or he would like jump on me for stuff

00:33:36.820 --> 00:33:39.470
and I was like, "I'm already
better than you, I'm 12.

00:33:40.697 --> 00:33:41.810
"What are you doing, dad?"

00:33:41.810 --> 00:33:44.760
So it was a balance of
having both types of parents,

00:33:44.760 --> 00:33:47.180
but I think ultimately the job of a parent

00:33:47.180 --> 00:33:48.621
is to prepare you for life,

00:33:48.621 --> 00:33:51.040
and losing is a part of life,

00:33:51.040 --> 00:33:52.630
it's just what you do with that loss.

00:33:52.630 --> 00:33:53.920
Do you use it to make you better

00:33:53.920 --> 00:33:55.590
or do you use it to make you feel like

00:33:55.590 --> 00:33:57.373
you aren't worthy of love anymore?

00:33:57.373 --> 00:33:58.206
And that's with them.

00:33:58.206 --> 00:34:03.130
- I'm super fascinated by
the absolute value of losing.

00:34:04.230 --> 00:34:06.120
I'm just super into it,

00:34:06.120 --> 00:34:10.900
and I'm just shocked by
the lack of self-awareness

00:34:10.900 --> 00:34:15.900
of parents to realize it's
doing such a huge disservice.

00:34:16.030 --> 00:34:19.060
I just see so many of my
friends and contemporaries,

00:34:19.060 --> 00:34:22.750
like, it's just so obvious that the fear

00:34:22.750 --> 00:34:26.840
of losing just cripples
them because they never--

00:34:26.840 --> 00:34:29.590
Like, it's super important
to taste that shit early on.

00:34:30.760 --> 00:34:32.010
Interesting.

00:34:32.010 --> 00:34:34.733
- And taste the result of that, you know?

00:34:35.640 --> 00:34:37.330
The best thing that you can do,

00:34:37.330 --> 00:34:39.840
as any parent or any mentor or anybody,

00:34:39.840 --> 00:34:41.660
is take the worst possible thing,

00:34:41.660 --> 00:34:44.160
the thing that's the curse,
the things that's the loss,

00:34:44.160 --> 00:34:48.447
and say, "Now, how do you
become grateful for this?

00:34:48.447 --> 00:34:50.090
"How is this going to make you better?"

00:34:50.090 --> 00:34:53.490
And you teach that, that's
the fucking recipe for life.

00:34:53.490 --> 00:34:55.050
Whatever failure, whatever loss,

00:34:55.050 --> 00:34:58.110
whatever curse, whatever hardship,

00:34:58.110 --> 00:35:00.440
use that thing to make
you that much stronger,

00:35:00.440 --> 00:35:01.273
teach that shit.

00:35:01.273 --> 00:35:04.223
- Do you feel that you're
over-reliant on Instagram?

00:35:05.320 --> 00:35:07.600
So this is something I wanna
spend a lot of time on,

00:35:07.600 --> 00:35:09.690
it's the first time I'm bringing it out,

00:35:09.690 --> 00:35:11.413
it's super important for me.

00:35:14.100 --> 00:35:15.280
I've got better sense for you,

00:35:15.280 --> 00:35:17.610
and I think you're more diversified.

00:35:17.610 --> 00:35:19.900
You know, as people are clearly trying

00:35:19.900 --> 00:35:22.060
to figure out their value in the--

00:35:22.060 --> 00:35:23.580
Thank God for influencer market,

00:35:23.580 --> 00:35:25.983
listen to your two intros, right?

00:35:28.900 --> 00:35:30.750
You know, it's really fun to critique

00:35:30.750 --> 00:35:32.930
the parents of the world right now

00:35:32.930 --> 00:35:36.280
and we just had a lot of
fun razzing not ourselves,

00:35:36.280 --> 00:35:39.400
do you believe, very simple yes, no,

00:35:39.400 --> 00:35:41.960
do you believe that you're
too vulnerable to Instagram?

00:35:41.960 --> 00:35:43.260
- Yes and no.

00:35:43.260 --> 00:35:44.400
I mean, that's the thing.

00:35:44.400 --> 00:35:49.100
I've done basically my same
job for 20 years or so,

00:35:49.100 --> 00:35:51.250
and like he mentioned, you know,

00:35:51.250 --> 00:35:54.749
we're both in unique sports that don't--

00:35:54.749 --> 00:35:55.582
- [Gary] They're not mainstream.

00:35:55.582 --> 00:35:58.750
- They're not mainstream
and I'm also coming off of,

00:35:58.750 --> 00:36:01.430
you know, a career of competing as well.

00:36:01.430 --> 00:36:03.260
So yeah, I mean--

00:36:03.260 --> 00:36:04.683
- So you retired a year ago?

00:36:05.820 --> 00:36:07.730
- I still do events but not full-time.

00:36:07.730 --> 00:36:08.940
- And did that feel weird?

00:36:08.940 --> 00:36:11.020
Was that like a detox or was that gradual?

00:36:11.020 --> 00:36:12.010
Did you feel like that was happening

00:36:12.010 --> 00:36:13.030
over the last three or four years?

00:36:13.030 --> 00:36:14.610
- I felt like it was gradual,

00:36:14.610 --> 00:36:16.920
and like I mentioned I like to win,

00:36:16.920 --> 00:36:21.420
so where I felt like I
couldn't dedicate my time and--

00:36:21.420 --> 00:36:22.480
- At the highest levels?

00:36:22.480 --> 00:36:24.340
- Yeah, like I don't
wanna show up to events

00:36:24.340 --> 00:36:26.920
and not do good or not
feel like I gave it my all,

00:36:26.920 --> 00:36:28.307
so that's kind of where I was at.

00:36:28.307 --> 00:36:30.558
- [Gary] You lost the
energy for it, makes sense.

00:36:30.558 --> 00:36:31.700
- But, you know, I still think about it.

00:36:31.700 --> 00:36:34.530
I still have, you know, surf
meets in the Olympics for 2020,

00:36:34.530 --> 00:36:35.820
that's something that's--

00:36:35.820 --> 00:36:36.653
- Is it the first time?

00:36:36.653 --> 00:36:37.550
- First time, yeah.

00:36:37.550 --> 00:36:38.660
- [Gary] Are you like, "Fuck."

00:36:38.660 --> 00:36:41.500
- No, I literally tell
a story where I cried

00:36:41.500 --> 00:36:43.190
when I heard I got in.

00:36:43.190 --> 00:36:44.040
- I couldn't imagine.

00:36:44.040 --> 00:36:45.640
- I was like, "Wow."

00:36:45.640 --> 00:36:46.970
- [Gary] The timing's super weird.

00:36:46.970 --> 00:36:49.333
- Yeah, it is weird.

00:36:49.333 --> 00:36:52.158
- It was '16 or if it was '24,

00:36:52.158 --> 00:36:53.030
it would've been better for you?

00:36:53.030 --> 00:36:56.713
- Yeah, I mean, and also I
think what's interesting to me,

00:36:56.713 --> 00:37:00.240
I even, ten, twenty years
ago when I was competing,

00:37:00.240 --> 00:37:03.920
we barely had a girl's tour or anything

00:37:03.920 --> 00:37:05.650
and now it's, like, an Olympic sport

00:37:05.650 --> 00:37:08.160
and I'm, you know, very grateful

00:37:08.160 --> 00:37:11.410
and I feel like I
contributed to a lot of--

00:37:11.410 --> 00:37:15.270
Me and another few top surfers
to get surfing out there.

00:37:15.270 --> 00:37:17.260
- Meanwhile, you can't help
but running through your head,

00:37:17.260 --> 00:37:20.347
like, "Fuck, I could've
won an Olympic gold medal."

00:37:20.347 --> 00:37:22.253
- You never know, it's on my mind.

00:37:23.253 --> 00:37:25.080
- But you would have to legitimately--

00:37:25.080 --> 00:37:26.580
- [Anastasia] Quit everything.

00:37:28.010 --> 00:37:29.560
- If you're planning on doing it,

00:37:29.560 --> 00:37:32.270
you probably have to
leave right this second.

00:37:32.270 --> 00:37:33.679
- Exactly, I'll see you guys in two years.

00:37:33.679 --> 00:37:36.117
Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:37:36.117 --> 00:37:37.630
Which it runs through my mind.

00:37:37.630 --> 00:37:39.820
I'm like, "Hmm," you know?

00:37:39.820 --> 00:37:40.870
- On a very serious note,

00:37:40.870 --> 00:37:42.810
if you literally walked
out of this room right now

00:37:42.810 --> 00:37:45.850
and went to Hawaii and started
getting your shit together,

00:37:45.850 --> 00:37:47.043
you know, it's tough.

00:37:49.564 --> 00:37:52.550
People are so confused by
the difference between,

00:37:52.550 --> 00:37:56.610
like, you fooling around
with your friends and what--

00:37:56.610 --> 00:37:59.000
I actually think that is one of the most

00:37:59.000 --> 00:38:00.890
fascinating things to me in my life,

00:38:00.890 --> 00:38:05.770
that regular people can't
comprehend the delta

00:38:05.770 --> 00:38:10.253
between themselves and
the tippy top of a sport.

00:38:11.857 --> 00:38:14.223
Like, my friends always say dumb shit,

00:38:15.720 --> 00:38:16.940
if we play five-on-five pickup ball

00:38:16.940 --> 00:38:19.250
and when you get the right five together

00:38:19.250 --> 00:38:20.490
and you, like, really feel it,

00:38:20.490 --> 00:38:21.960
and then somebody says something stupid

00:38:21.960 --> 00:38:23.820
because you won, like,
seven games in a row,

00:38:23.820 --> 00:38:26.440
like, "You, if we played the Warriors--"

00:38:26.440 --> 00:38:28.487
Like, "When are you gonna score, dick?"

00:38:29.850 --> 00:38:30.720
Forget about winning,

00:38:30.720 --> 00:38:33.210
we're not even gonna score
if they really tried.

00:38:33.210 --> 00:38:35.313
Score in a basketball game.

00:38:38.900 --> 00:38:40.247
I always tell my friends, I'm like,

00:38:40.247 --> 00:38:43.497
"Bro, look at all the NCAA athletes,

00:38:43.497 --> 00:38:44.727
"look at the ones that are like

00:38:44.727 --> 00:38:49.337
"the seventh best player
in college at the time

00:38:49.337 --> 00:38:51.590
"that don't even make
it to the next level."

00:38:51.590 --> 00:38:54.590
The delta is so extreme.

00:38:54.590 --> 00:38:56.130
Do you think that if you walked outta here

00:38:56.130 --> 00:38:57.860
right now and started going hardcore,

00:38:57.860 --> 00:39:00.330
that you could make a real run in 2020?

00:39:00.330 --> 00:39:01.882
- It would be a struggle.

00:39:01.882 --> 00:39:02.740
It would be a struggle.

00:39:02.740 --> 00:39:05.490
And that's where it's like, "Ugh."

00:39:07.040 --> 00:39:08.110
- If you thought you could medal,

00:39:08.110 --> 00:39:09.410
you'd probably walk out of here right now.

00:39:09.410 --> 00:39:10.840
Because it's cool, right?

00:39:10.840 --> 00:39:11.740
- Yeah, well yeah.

00:39:13.960 --> 00:39:15.000
- I apologize for cutting you off.

00:39:15.000 --> 00:39:16.710
I notice Lewis Howes has a quote here,

00:39:16.710 --> 00:39:18.900
Lewis went to the Olympics
on a handball thing,

00:39:18.900 --> 00:39:20.940
I still think it's the funniest shit.

00:39:20.940 --> 00:39:22.700
Like, literally, when he did that,

00:39:22.700 --> 00:39:24.177
there was one flight where I was like,

00:39:24.177 --> 00:39:27.520
"What's something super random
that's in the Olympics?"

00:39:27.520 --> 00:39:31.460
Like, something dumb as shit
that nobody knows about.

00:39:31.460 --> 00:39:34.610
I was, like, looking for the
hack to get into the Olympics

00:39:34.610 --> 00:39:37.840
because I think it's just
funny and it's amazing.

00:39:37.840 --> 00:39:39.560
Got it, so that's kinda how
it runs through your mind.

00:39:39.560 --> 00:39:42.433
- Yeah, and to further
answer your question,

00:39:43.370 --> 00:39:46.720
speaking back to Instagram and, you know,

00:39:46.720 --> 00:39:48.520
working with brands and stuff--

00:39:48.520 --> 00:39:50.663
- [Gary] Look, what's your
second biggest platform?

00:39:51.730 --> 00:39:52.990
- Facebook's actually my number one,

00:39:52.990 --> 00:39:56.632
but, I mean, Facebook,
Instagram, I do them all,

00:39:56.632 --> 00:39:57.731
Twitter, Snapshot,

00:39:57.731 --> 00:40:00.870
but it's like anything.

00:40:00.870 --> 00:40:04.027
Like, I think every day,
"Is it gonna change?"

00:40:06.483 --> 00:40:09.340
I just saw Myspace Tom
literally yesterday in LA.

00:40:09.340 --> 00:40:11.427
I know him, he's a nice
guy, and I was like,

00:40:11.427 --> 00:40:12.847
"Myspace Tom, what's up?"

00:40:13.781 --> 00:40:15.330
- Do you think he appreciated you saying

00:40:15.330 --> 00:40:16.930
Myspace Tom or did you just say Tom?

00:40:16.930 --> 00:40:18.068
- I say both.

00:40:18.068 --> 00:40:20.840
I literally might go, "Yo,
you're, like, Myspace Tom,"

00:40:20.840 --> 00:40:22.307
and I'm like, "This is great."

00:40:22.307 --> 00:40:24.132
But I was on Myspace back in the day,

00:40:24.132 --> 00:40:25.873
I was a really geeky kid.

00:40:26.730 --> 00:40:28.210
- You also were building a brand at five,

00:40:28.210 --> 00:40:30.044
so that makes sense.

00:40:30.044 --> 00:40:31.670
Do you feel like you're
over-reliant on Instagram?

00:40:31.670 --> 00:40:32.503
- Yes.

00:40:33.600 --> 00:40:35.420
- Do you even have a number two?

00:40:35.420 --> 00:40:36.253
- Probably Twitter.

00:40:36.253 --> 00:40:37.790
- But that's like a distant two, right?

00:40:37.790 --> 00:40:38.623
- It's probably a fourth, yeah,

00:40:38.623 --> 00:40:40.334
of my following on Instagram.

00:40:40.334 --> 00:40:42.335
- And so how do you think about that?

00:40:42.335 --> 00:40:44.620
Do you think about podcasts,
do you think about YouTube?

00:40:44.620 --> 00:40:46.070
- Yeah, I think, like I
said, right now I'm more

00:40:46.070 --> 00:40:47.830
focused on that more than ever.

00:40:47.830 --> 00:40:49.760
I think I'm at a point in my
life where I'm comfortable

00:40:49.760 --> 00:40:52.510
and wanna start getting
into different things.

00:40:52.510 --> 00:40:54.680
- Comfortable because you
scratched the itch of lacrosse

00:40:54.680 --> 00:40:56.060
or comfortable because
you're starting to get

00:40:56.060 --> 00:40:57.793
some deals and, like, can see it?

00:40:58.637 --> 00:40:59.470
Number two?

00:40:59.470 --> 00:41:01.260
- I have some deals and I'm comfortable

00:41:01.260 --> 00:41:03.140
where I'm at in the sport.

00:41:03.140 --> 00:41:06.040
You know, I'm always
trying to be the best,

00:41:06.040 --> 00:41:08.350
but I'm, you know,
confident in my position

00:41:08.350 --> 00:41:09.250
right now and where I am,

00:41:09.250 --> 00:41:12.420
and I wanna start really
taking that next step.

00:41:12.420 --> 00:41:15.820
So I'm looking at
podcasts, YouTube channel,

00:41:15.820 --> 00:41:17.610
seeing what I can do best,

00:41:17.610 --> 00:41:20.250
what I can do with the most consistency

00:41:20.250 --> 00:41:22.260
because I think that's
what I admire the most

00:41:22.260 --> 00:41:24.470
about people who are in this
space and who do it great,

00:41:24.470 --> 00:41:25.920
is how consistent they are.

00:41:25.920 --> 00:41:27.680
And, you know, you're putting
out podcasts every day

00:41:27.680 --> 00:41:29.630
and that takes effort, that takes work,

00:41:29.630 --> 00:41:32.330
so what's my next go-to platform

00:41:32.330 --> 00:41:33.900
that I can be consistent in?

00:41:33.900 --> 00:41:36.830
I wanna be a consistent performer
like I can be in lacrosse,

00:41:36.830 --> 00:41:39.490
I wanna do that with what I choose next,

00:41:39.490 --> 00:41:41.260
and for now, right now,
Instagram's the one

00:41:41.260 --> 00:41:42.790
that I can be most consistent with.

00:41:42.790 --> 00:41:43.623
- Aubrey, how about for you?

00:41:43.623 --> 00:41:46.810
And how do you think about you as a human

00:41:46.810 --> 00:41:49.120
versus Onnit the brand?

00:41:49.120 --> 00:41:52.390
- That's a funny thing to kinda
reconcile at a certain point

00:41:52.390 --> 00:41:53.750
because it was birthed out of me,

00:41:53.750 --> 00:41:54.960
it was just me.

00:41:54.960 --> 00:41:57.180
Me and Onnit were smashed together as one

00:41:57.180 --> 00:41:59.360
and now 180 people in there,

00:41:59.360 --> 00:42:02.730
and it has a life of
its own and it's my job

00:42:02.730 --> 00:42:05.430
to just kind of shepherd it
and steer it in the right ways

00:42:05.430 --> 00:42:07.260
that uphold certain principles of mine,

00:42:07.260 --> 00:42:08.990
but it's its own thing for sure

00:42:08.990 --> 00:42:12.650
and it can get kinda trippy
to look at this thing

00:42:12.650 --> 00:42:15.090
that was like a piece of my flesh,

00:42:15.090 --> 00:42:18.290
that now has developed into
something that's no longer me.

00:42:18.290 --> 00:42:20.640
- What's the biggest
challenge of something

00:42:20.640 --> 00:42:22.190
that is a piece of your flesh.

00:42:22.190 --> 00:42:25.220
that is now a company of 180 people.

00:42:25.220 --> 00:42:26.360
For you today.

00:42:26.360 --> 00:42:27.780
How many years ago did it start?

00:42:27.780 --> 00:42:30.220
- 2011 was really when it got going.

00:42:30.220 --> 00:42:33.253
- So, like, you know, what's
the biggest challenge for you?

00:42:34.626 --> 00:42:37.190
At this point because I think
you're in an interesting spot,

00:42:37.190 --> 00:42:38.740
I have a lot of empathy for it.

00:42:39.680 --> 00:42:41.440
A lot of times we talk about, you know,

00:42:41.440 --> 00:42:43.270
so much of my content
is geared towards people

00:42:43.270 --> 00:42:45.866
that haven't started yet or you're in.

00:42:45.866 --> 00:42:49.960
You, I, we're past those
places in our lives.

00:42:49.960 --> 00:42:52.330
I feel like you're in a place where your

00:42:52.330 --> 00:42:57.140
personal brand is gaining moment, right?

00:42:57.140 --> 00:42:59.310
You have been focused on Onnit.

00:42:59.310 --> 00:43:01.440
You know, listen, I always
try to remind people,

00:43:01.440 --> 00:43:05.423
like, I built Wine Library
for the first decade.

00:43:07.501 --> 00:43:09.490
No, I got lucky, let's call it what it is.

00:43:09.490 --> 00:43:12.360
There was no putting out
content, you know, back then,

00:43:12.360 --> 00:43:14.780
but there was blogs and
I can't write for shit

00:43:14.780 --> 00:43:15.613
so that helped me,

00:43:15.613 --> 00:43:17.980
but Myspace, I watched very carefully,

00:43:17.980 --> 00:43:20.110
Friendster and Myspace, I was fascinated.

00:43:20.110 --> 00:43:22.380
I didn't start content on there.

00:43:22.380 --> 00:43:25.640
You know, I think it's
an interesting transition

00:43:25.640 --> 00:43:27.630
when you actually run a company

